diff --git a/PUBLIC-STATS.md b/PUBLIC-STATS.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c2c304d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/PUBLIC-STATS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,106 @@
+# Public aggregate statistics (local candidate)
+
+This patch extends **halo-sigs/plugin-umami v1.5.0**, commit
+`196a36de74da490436fec7e09edef65f47d6f4d2`. Candidate version is
+`1.5.0-next.1`, requiring **Halo 2.27.0**. It is not an upstream release.
+The added API targets **self-hosted Umami 3.4.0** only; Umami Cloud, older APIs,
+real account reconciliation and production deployment are not yet accepted.
+
+## Configuration
+
+1. Configure the existing Umami site URL and website UUID in plugin settings.
+   The optional console share link is not required for this API.
+   The URL must use HTTPS (HTTP is allowed only for localhost/127.0.0.1/::1
+   local verification). Do not append `/api`; optional installation subpaths work.
+2. Create an Umami API key for an account that can view the selected website.
+   Inject it as `HALO_UMAMI_STATS_API_KEY` into the **Halo process environment**,
+   then restart Halo. Never put it in the theme, plugin ConfigMap or a public URL.
+   The existing plugin viewing role can read its ConfigMap; a password input alone
+   would not protect credentials. Rotation uses the same environment/deployment
+   mechanism. No username/password or login-token refresh is implemented.
+3. Set `basic.statsStartAt` to the real UTC collection start, e.g.
+   `2026-01-01T00:00:00Z`, then enable `basic.publicStatsEnabled` (default false).
+4. Configure the theme's public statistics URL as
+   `/apis/api.umami.halo.run/v1alpha1/stats`.
+5. If the theme already injects Umami tracking, enable `basic.disableTracker`
+   to stop this plugin injecting a second browser tracker. It does not disable
+   RSS telemetry. There must be only one browser tracking owner. The plugin
+   injects no browser script while the endpoint, website ID or script name is
+   missing/empty/blank, so an unconfigured plugin cannot emit `null/umami.js`.
+
+Anonymous GET accepts **only** optional `path`, once:
+
+```
+GET /apis/api.umami.halo.run/v1alpha1/stats
+{"site_uv":12,"site_pv":34}
+GET /apis/api.umami.halo.run/v1alpha1/stats?path=%2Farchives%2Fexample%2F
+{"site_uv":12,"site_pv":34,"page_pv":5}
+```
+
+No client-controlled endpoint, website, date range, API key or arbitrary filter
+is accepted. `path` is a canonical leading-slash URL pathname, up to 1024
+characters; no query, fragment, control characters, backslash, dot traversal,
+leading `//` or comma. Segments equal to `.` or `..` are rejected, including
+case-insensitive `%2e` encodings and mixtures of literal/encoded dots; this
+explicitly covers leading `/../`, which Java URI normalization preserves.
+Other percent bytes remain untouched (e.g. `%25`, `%E4%BD%A0`, `%2e-report`).
+Commas are rejected because Umami 3.4 equality filters
+interpret them as a list. Percent-encoded URL path bytes are preserved, as are
+trailing slash and case. No path decoding, slash folding or query aggregation
+is invented by this adapter. Umami receives an explicit `eq.<path>` filter.
+
+## Meaning and limits
+
+`site_pv` maps to Umami `pageviews`; `site_uv` maps to its `visitors` for the
+entire configured UTC start-to-refresh interval. `page_pv` is `pageviews` for
+the exact path in the same configured start-to-refresh interval. UV is **not**
+a sum of daily UV and is not a count of identifiable people. Umami website
+reset timestamps can shorten the effective range. The adapter does not recover
+deleted or uncollected history or guarantee matching a differently filtered
+Umami dashboard.
+
+Successful results and failures are memoized for up to 60 seconds, including
+in-flight coalescing. Site results are reused across page requests; their
+refresh time can differ from a page result by up to 60 seconds. There are at
+most 256 cache keys and 60 newly admitted upstream requests per minute per
+Halo process. This is a service-wide upstream budget, not a per-IP quota;
+uncached paths may receive 429 under load. Cached requests remain available.
+The total upstream request timeout is 3 seconds, below the theme's 5 seconds.
+Response buffering is limited to 16 KiB. Redirects are not followed by the
+default WebClient connector. No stale-success fallback or invented zero is used.
+
+The public endpoint returns 404 when disabled, 400 for invalid query/path,
+429 with Retry-After for budget exhaustion, and generic 503 for missing/invalid
+configuration, authentication failure, timeout, non-2xx response or invalid
+counts. Upstream error bodies and credentials are not returned. All responses
+are no-store; caching is internal. It publishes only non-negative JavaScript
+safe integer aggregate values, never visitor records.
+
+## Pinned API references
+
+- https://github.com/umami-software/umami/blob/v3.4.0/src/app/api/websites/%5BwebsiteId%5D/stats/route.ts
+- https://github.com/umami-software/umami/blob/v3.4.0/src/queries/sql/getWebsiteStats.ts
+- https://github.com/umami-software/umami/blob/v3.4.0/src/lib/params.ts
+- https://docs.umami.is/docs/api/authentication
+
+## Build and verification
+
+Use JDK 21 and the Gradle 9.5.0 wrapper. Existing console sources retain their
+locked dependencies; use pnpm 9.15.9 for this upstream checkout.
+
+```
+corepack pnpm@9.15.9 --dir console install --frozen-lockfile
+corepack pnpm@9.15.9 --dir console run build
+./gradlew clean test build -x buildFrontend
+```
+
+`-x buildFrontend` is valid only after the explicit console build above;
+without it the upstream Gradle task invokes whichever pnpm is on PATH.
+For backend-only development: `./gradlew test -x buildFrontend`.
+
+Tests cover mapping, fixed target/credentials, strict path encoding, unsafe
+counts, default-off, configuration invalidation, cache expiry, bounded upstream
+rate, concurrency coalescing, secret-safe failures and the HTTP contract.
+A mocked API is not real-account acceptance. Before production, reconcile a
+known account and pages against Umami's same UTC window, test key revocation,
+renewal, plugin disable/re-enable, and ensure tracker ownership is singular.
